[
  "Use HTTPS to protect client-server communication\nWHAT: Failing to use HTTPS (SSL/TLS) for client-server communication exposes data to interception and tampering.\nWHY: Attackers on the network can read or modify sensitive information (credentials, session tokens, personal data) and perform man-in-the-middle attacks.\nHOW: Always terminate connections over HTTPS using valid SSL/TLS certificates (e.g., from a trusted CA), configure your application server or reverse proxy (e.g., Express https module, NGINX, HAProxy) to require HTTPS, and disable plain HTTP or redirect it to HTTPS.\nEXAMPLE: Before (insecure): `http.createServer(app).listen(80);` After (secure): `https.createServer({ key: fs.readFileSync('privkey.pem'), cert: fs.readFileSync('fullchain.pem') }, app).listen(443);`\n",
  "Use secure connections, authentication, access control, and configuration\nWHAT: Failing to use SSL/TLS, secure comparisons, cryptographically secure randomness, strong authentication, least-privilege access control, and hardened configuration leads to data exposure, account takeover, and infrastructure compromise.\nWHY: Attackers can perform MITM, timing, and prediction attacks; brute-force or abuse weak auth; escalate privileges; and exploit misconfigured networks, cookies, and services.\nHOW: Enforce HTTPS with SSL/TLS everywhere; use crypto.timingSafeEqual for secret/hash comparison; use crypto.randomBytes for tokens; require MFA/2FA, strong passwords, no default creds, standard auth protocols, and auth rate limiting; apply least privilege, avoid root, use role/service accounts and group-based permissions; restrict internal network access, expose no internal services publicly, harden cookies with Secure/SameSite/HttpOnly, and protect VPCs with strict rules and DDoS protections.\nEXAMPLE (Node.js, HTTPS & timing-safe compare):\nBefore: `http.createServer(...); if (hmac === provided) {...}`\nAfter: `https.createServer({key, cert}, ...); if (crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(hmac), Buffer.from(provided))) {...}`\n",
  "Sensitive data and component security\nWHAT: Applications expose sensitive data (PII, secrets, passwords, tokens) or run with vulnerable components, weak transport, or insufficient logging/monitoring, leading to data theft or undetected compromise.\nWHY: Attackers can steal or modify confidential data, hijack accounts, exploit known vulnerabilities, or evade detection, causing regulatory, financial, and reputational damage.\nHOW: Enforce TLS-only connections with HSTS; segment networks and deny unnecessary egress; store secrets in managed vaults and never in logs or plaintext; encrypt PII and all data crossing trust boundaries; regularly scan images/dependencies and auto-patch OS; use short-lived access tokens with refresh tokens; enable and alert on detailed audit logs; use XSS-safe templating, context-aware escaping/encoding, and CSP; comply with local data-privacy laws; and publish security.txt/SECURITY.md with clear vulnerability-reporting contacts.\nEXAMPLE: Before (insecure): log.info(\"User login: email=\" + email + \", pwd=\" + password); After (secure): log.info(\"User login attempt for email={}\", emailMasked); // never log passwords or secrets.\n",
  "Use security-focused linters and pre-commit checks\nWHAT: Failing to use security-focused linters and git hooks allows unsafe patterns (e.g., eval on user input, non-literal fs paths, weak randomness, unsafe regex) and secrets to enter the codebase unnoticed.\nWHY: These issues can lead to code execution, path traversal, denial of service, weak cryptography, or credential leakage in production.\nHOW: Enable and enforce security plugins (e.g., eslint-plugin-security, tslint-config-security) in CI and local dev, configure rules for unsafe APIs and patterns, and add pre-commit/pre-push hooks to block commits with violations or secrets.\nEXAMPLE: Before: `const userCode = req.body.code; eval(userCode);` After: `// disallow eval via linter; use a vetted sandbox or a restricted interpreter instead, and fail the build if eval is used.`\n",
  "Output Escaping for Untrusted Data in Web Contexts\nWHAT: Untrusted data rendered into HTML, JavaScript, CSS, URLs, or event-handler attributes without context-appropriate escaping can be interpreted as executable code (e.g., injected `<script>` tags) and executed by the browser.\nWHY: This enables cross-site scripting (XSS), allowing attackers to run arbitrary scripts in users’ browsers, steal data, hijack sessions, or modify page content.\nHOW: Treat all user-controlled or external data as untrusted; before inserting it into any web document, apply context-specific escaping/encoding using a vetted security-focused library (e.g., HTML-escape for HTML body, attribute-escape for attributes, JS-escape for JS strings, CSS-escape for CSS, URL-encode for URLs) and never place raw untrusted data directly in `<script>`, event handlers, CSS, or tag/attribute names.\nEXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `<div>${userComment}</div>`; After (safe): `<div>${escapeHtml(userComment)}</div>` using a security-focused escaping library.\n",
  "Child process command injection via unsanitized input\nWHAT: Unsanitized or unvalidated user input is concatenated into child process commands (e.g., shell strings), allowing injection of arbitrary shell metacharacters and commands.\nWHY: Attackers can achieve remote code execution, access or exfiltrate sensitive system data, and cause data loss or full system compromise.\nHOW: Avoid passing user-controlled data to shell-based APIs; if unavoidable, strictly validate against an allowlist and escape/quote arguments, use non-shell exec APIs that take argument arrays, run with least-privilege user/group IDs, and isolate processes (e.g., containers, chroot, sandboxes).\nEXAMPLE: Before (Node.js): exec('\"/path/to/script.sh\" --opt ' + userInput); After: execFile('/path/to/script.sh', ['--opt', safeArg], { uid: lowPrivUid, gid: lowPrivGid });\n",
  "Hide detailed error information from clients\nWHAT: Returning full error details or stack traces to clients (e.g., via default error handlers) exposes internal paths, modules, and workflows.\nWHY: Attackers can use this information to map the system, discover technologies in use, and craft targeted exploits.\nHOW: In production, configure a centralized error handler that logs full errors server-side but returns only generic messages and appropriate HTTP status codes to clients; ensure environment flags (e.g., NODE_ENV) are set correctly.\nEXAMPLE: Before (Express, leaking stack): `app.use(errorHandler());` with default dev config; After: `app.use((err, req, res, next) => { console.error(err); res.status(err.status||500).json({error:'Internal server error'}); });`\n",
  "Unsafe redirects in Node.js/Express\nWHAT: Using unvalidated, user-controlled URLs (e.g., `req.query.url`) as redirect targets can cause open redirect vulnerabilities.\nWHY: Attackers can trick users into visiting malicious sites that appear legitimate, enabling phishing, credential theft, and other attacks.\nHOW: Never redirect directly to user input; instead, restrict redirects to same-origin paths or a strict allowlist of full URLs, and normalize/validate the target before calling `res.redirect`.\nEXAMPLE:\nBefore: `res.redirect(req.query.url);`\nAfter: `res.redirect(getValidRedirect(req.query.url));` where `getValidRedirect` only allows relative paths or URLs from a predefined whitelist.\n",
  "Dynamic module and file loading from user-controlled input\nWHAT: Loading modules or accessing files using paths derived from user-controlled variables (e.g., parameters, query strings) can cause arbitrary file or code loading.\nWHY: Attackers can read or execute unintended files, leading to code execution, data exposure, or privilege escalation.\nHOW: Do not pass user-controlled values directly to require/import or file APIs; instead, use fixed paths, a strict allowlist of known-safe identifiers mapped to fixed paths, and validate/normalize any dynamic components before use.\nEXAMPLE: Before (insecure): `const helper = require(helperPath);` After (secure): `const helper = require('./helpers/upload');`\n",
  "Secure session cookie configuration in web frameworks\nWHAT: Default session middleware settings (e.g., express-session’s default cookie name and cookie.secure=false) expose predictable session identifiers and allow session cookies over insecure HTTP.\nWHY: Predictable session names aid framework fingerprinting and targeted exploits, while non-secure cookies are vulnerable to theft via man-in-the-middle attacks and can lead to session hijacking.\nHOW: Override defaults by setting a unique, non-framework-specific session name, enabling cookie.secure for HTTPS-only transport, and enabling httpOnly and reasonable maxAge on session cookies.\nEXAMPLE:\nBefore (insecure): app.use(session({ secret: 's', name: 'connect.sid', cookie: { secure: false } }));\nAfter (hardened): app.use(session({ secret: 'youruniquesecret', name: 'youruniquename', cookie: { httpOnly: true, secure: true, maxAge: 60000*60*24 } }));\n",
  "Rate limiting to prevent request flooding\nWHAT: Failure to enforce rate limiting on incoming requests allows attackers or misbehaving clients to overwhelm the application with excessive concurrent or rapid requests.\nWHY: This can enable brute‑force attacks, facilitate DDoS conditions, and exhaust server resources, leading to degraded performance or downtime.\nHOW: Implement rate limiting at the edge (e.g., nginx, load balancer) or via middleware (e.g., rate-limiter-flexible, express-rate-limit), using client IP or other identifiers; configure reasonable limits, block durations, and ensure `trust proxy` is enabled when behind a proxy so the correct client IP is used.\nEXAMPLE: In Express.js, use `app.enable('trust proxy'); const apiLimiter = rateLimit({ windowMs: 15*60*1000, max: 100 }); app.use('/user/', apiLimiter);` to cap requests per client for sensitive routes.\n",
  "Avoid leaking secrets in npm packages\nWHAT: Secrets (e.g., .env, config, CI files) can be accidentally published to public npm registries when .npmignore, .gitignore, and the package.json files array are misconfigured or out of sync.\nWHY: Publishing secrets exposes credentials and internal configuration, enabling account takeover, data exfiltration, or infrastructure compromise.\nHOW: Explicitly list sensitive files/directories in .npmignore, prefer a restrictive files array in package.json as a whitelist, keep .gitignore and .npmignore aligned, and always run npm publish --dry-run to verify the final package contents before publishing.\nEXAMPLE: Before: relying only on .gitignore and omitting .env from .npmignore, causing .env to be published. After: add .env and other secret-bearing files to .npmignore and/or include only built artifacts (e.g., dist/*.js) in the package.json files array, then confirm with npm publish --dry-run.\n",
  "Regular expression denial of service (ReDoS) in single-threaded environments\nWHAT: Using vulnerable regular expressions (e.g., with nested or repeated capturing groups like `(a|aa)+` or `([a-zA-Z]+)*`) on user-controlled input can cause exponential-time matching and block the event loop, making the application unresponsive.\nWHY: An attacker can send specially crafted input that triggers worst-case regex behavior, leading to CPU exhaustion, denial of service, and loss of availability.\nHOW: Avoid complex custom regex for validation when possible; prefer well-reviewed validation libraries (e.g., `validator.js`). If regex is required, statically analyze patterns with tools like `safe-regex`, disallow nested/repeated groups on untrusted input, and test patterns against worst-case inputs before deployment.\nEXAMPLE:\nBefore (unsafe): `app.post('/signup', (req,res)=>{ if(!emailRegex.test(req.body.email)) return res.status(400).end(); });`\nAfter (safer): `app.post('/signup', (req,res)=>{ if(!validator.isEmail(req.body.email)) return res.status(400).end(); });`\n",
  "HTTP security headers configuration\nWHAT: Missing or misconfigured HTTP security headers (HSTS, HPKP, X-Frame-Options, X-XSS-Protection, etc.) can leave applications vulnerable to downgrade attacks, cookie hijacking, clickjacking, and XSS, even when other code is correct.\nWHY: Without these headers, attackers can perform man-in-the-middle, UI redress, or script-injection attacks, leading to credential theft, account takeover, or data exposure.\nHOW: In your web server or framework, set and maintain secure defaults for headers: enable HSTS with appropriate max-age and includeSubDomains; configure (or avoid) HPKP carefully; set X-Frame-Options to deny or sameorigin; and configure X-XSS-Protection or modern equivalents; use a maintained library (e.g., Helmet for Express/Koa) to centralize and version-control these settings.\nEXAMPLE: In Express (Node.js), use Helmet middleware and explicitly configure headers in one place, then verify via automated tests or CI checks that all HTTP responses include the required security headers with the intended values.\n",
  "Secure HTTP Response Headers Configuration\nWHAT: Missing or misconfigured security-related HTTP response headers (X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, Expect-CT, Content-Security-Policy) can allow content sniffing, excessive referrer leakage, certificate transparency bypass, and XSS/resource injection.\nWHY: Attackers can exploit these gaps to execute scripts, exfiltrate sensitive URLs, or abuse insecure TLS/CT configurations, leading to data exposure and account compromise.\nHOW: In server/framework config or middleware, set: `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`; an appropriate `Referrer-Policy` (e.g., `no-referrer` or `strict-origin-when-cross-origin`); `Expect-CT` with `max-age`, `enforce`, and `report-uri`; and a restrictive `Content-Security-Policy` (e.g., limit `script-src` to trusted origins).\nEXAMPLE (HTTP):\nBefore: (no security headers)\nAfter:\nX-Content-Type-Options: nosniff\nReferrer-Policy: no-referrer\nExpect-CT: max-age=2592000, enforce, report-uri=\"https://example.com/report-cert-transparency\"\nContent-Security-Policy: script-src 'self'\n",
  "Avoid dynamic code execution with eval-like functions\nWHAT: Executing strings as code using eval(), setTimeout(), setInterval(), or new Function() with data that can include user input can lead to arbitrary code execution.\nWHY: An attacker can run any JavaScript code with your process’s privileges, potentially compromising the server, data, and underlying system.\nHOW: Do not pass user-controlled or concatenated strings to these functions; refactor to use direct function calls, conditionals, or data-driven logic (e.g., lookup tables) instead of building code as strings.\nEXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `eval(userInput);` After (safer): `const actions = { delete: () => fs.rmSync(path) }; const action = actions[userInput]; if (action) action();`\n",
  "Validate JSON input with explicit schemas\nWHAT: Unvalidated or loosely validated JSON request bodies can contain unexpected fields, types, or sizes, leading to failures, DoS conditions, or unsafe deserialization behavior.\nWHY: Strict schema validation reduces attack surface and helps prevent issues like DoS (by rejecting oversized/complex payloads) and insecure deserialization (by disallowing unexpected structure/fields).\nHOW: Define strict JSON schemas (e.g., JSON Schema, joi) for each endpoint, including required fields, types, ranges, and length limits; run validation as early as possible (e.g., middleware) and reject invalid payloads with a 4xx error before business logic runs.\nEXAMPLE: Before (Node.js/Express): `router.post('/', (req,res)=>{ process(req.body); });` → After: `router.post('/', validator(Product.schema), (req,res)=>{ process(req.validatedBody); });` where `Product.schema` is a strict JSON schema.\n",
  "Third-party dependency vulnerability management\nWHAT: Using outdated or vulnerable third-party Node.js packages from npm/Yarn without checks can introduce known security flaws into your application.\nWHY: Attackers can exploit these known vulnerabilities to compromise confidentiality, integrity, or availability of your system.\nHOW: Regularly run automated dependency scanners (e.g., `npm audit`, Snyk) locally and in CI; review reports; promptly apply recommended patches or upgrades; and use services (e.g., Greenkeeper/renovation tools) to keep dependencies up to date.\nEXAMPLE: Before: no automated checks; dependencies updated ad hoc. After: CI pipeline runs `npm audit` and Snyk on each commit, fails the build on high-severity issues, and automatically opens pull requests to upgrade vulnerable packages.\n",
  "Run untrusted JavaScript in an isolated sandbox\nWHAT: Executing dynamically supplied or third-party JavaScript (e.g., custom loaders/plugins) directly in your main process can allow malicious code to access resources, crash the process, or run indefinitely.\nWHY: Untrusted code can exfiltrate data, corrupt state, cause denial of service, or compromise the host environment.\nHOW: Treat all dynamically provided JavaScript as untrusted; run it in a sandbox with strict isolation (separate process, serverless function, or hardened sandbox library), enforce timeouts and resource limits, and restrict accessible APIs and data.\nEXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `eval(userCode);` in the main process. After (safer, Node.js): run `userCode` in a dedicated child process or with a sandbox library (e.g., `vm2`, `sandbox`) configured with timeouts, no access to `process`, and a minimal, explicit API surface.\n",
  "JWT revocation and blacklist management\nWHAT: JWTs are stateless and remain valid until expiry, so a leaked or compromised token can still be used if there is no server-side revocation or blacklist mechanism, especially across multiple processes/instances.\nWHY: An attacker with a stolen token can impersonate a user and access protected resources until the token naturally expires, even after the user logs out or the account is suspected to be compromised.\nHOW: Implement a server-side revocation layer that tracks revoked/expired JWT IDs (e.g., `jti`) in a shared external store (Redis, Memcached, database) and check this blacklist on every authenticated request; avoid in-memory-only stores when running multiple processes.\nEXAMPLE: Before: verify JWT signature only and trust any valid token until expiry. After: on each request, verify signature and then query a shared blacklist store by `jti`; on logout or suspected compromise, add the token’s `jti` to the blacklist so subsequent requests are rejected.\n",
  "Secure password storage and hashing\nWHAT: Storing user passwords in plaintext or with weak/incorrect hashing (no salt, low work factor, wrong algorithm, predictable randomness) allows attackers to recover passwords if the database is compromised.\nWHY: An attacker who steals the password store can quickly brute-force or replay passwords, leading to account takeover and credential stuffing across other services.\nHOW: Always hash passwords with a dedicated password-hashing/KDF algorithm (e.g., bcrypt, scrypt, PBKDF2) using per-user unique salts and security-appropriate parameters (e.g., bcrypt cost ≥12, scrypt N≥32768/r=8/p=1, PBKDF2 iterations ≥10000); never store plaintext passwords; never use Math.random() or other non-cryptographic RNGs for salts or tokens.\nEXAMPLE: Before (insecure): `db.save({ user, password: pwd });` → After (secure, JS/bcrypt): `const saltRounds = 12; const hash = await bcrypt.hash(pwd, saltRounds); db.save({ user, passwordHash: hash });`\n",
  "Password hashing and salting for user credentials\nWHAT: Storing user passwords with weak or misconfigured hashing (e.g., no salt, low work factor, or raw/fast hashes) allows attackers to efficiently crack stolen password databases.\nWHY: Poor password storage enables large-scale brute force and reuse attacks across sites if hashes are leaked, compromising user accounts and potentially other services.\nHOW: Use a dedicated password hashing function (e.g., bcrypt, scrypt, PBKDF2, Argon2) with IETF/OWASP-recommended parameters; include a per-user, system-unique salt; tune cost/rounds/iterations to be as high as acceptable for your environment; avoid custom randomness and let the library generate salts.\nEXAMPLE: Server-side, hash = bcrypt.hash(password, workFactor, perUserSalt); store only hash + salt; on login, recompute with same parameters and compare; never store or log plaintext passwords.\n",
  "Password hashing and randomness usage\nWHAT: Using weak randomness sources (e.g., Math.random() or manual crypto.random() misuse) and non-iterated/simple hashing for passwords leads to predictable values and easily brute-forced password hashes.\nWHY: Predictable randomness and fast, single-iteration hashes allow attackers to efficiently guess passwords and may impact other processes relying on system entropy.\nHOW: Use established password-hashing algorithms (e.g., BCrypt/SCrypt) with appropriate iteration and hardness parameters, and rely on their built‑in secure randomness instead of generating your own random values where possible.\nEXAMPLE: Before (insecure): `const hash = crypto.subtle.digest('sha-256', password);` After (more secure): `const hash = await bcrypt.hash(password, workFactor);`\n",
  "Secure password storage\nWHAT: Storing passwords in plaintext or with weak/unsalted/fast hashes (e.g., MD5, SHA-1, unsalted SHA-256) allows attackers who access the database to recover user passwords.\nWHY: Compromised passwords enable account takeover, credential stuffing on other sites, and long-term user harm due to password reuse.\nHOW: Never store plaintext passwords; use a dedicated, slow password-hashing function (Argon2id, scrypt, bcrypt, or PBKDF2) with a unique, random salt per password, strong work factor (iterations/memory/cost), and secure random generator; do not use reversible encryption for passwords.\nEXAMPLE: Before (insecure): `stored = sha256(password)`; After (secure, language-agnostic pattern): `salt = SecureRandomBytes(); stored = PasswordHashFunction(password, salt, costParams); save(salt, stored)`.\n",
  "Database injection-safe data access\nWHAT: Database queries are built by concatenating or interpolating user-controlled input into SQL/NoSQL statements, allowing injection of arbitrary query logic or operators.\nWHY: Attackers can read/modify sensitive data or cause denial of service by executing unintended database operations or code inside the query engine.\nHOW: Never build queries via string concatenation with user input; validate and sanitize all incoming data using a dedicated validation library; use ORM/ODM/DAL libraries that enforce parameterized queries and data bindings for all dynamic values.\nEXAMPLE:\nBefore: `db.balances.find({ active: true, $where: (obj) => obj.credits - obj.debits < userInput });`\nAfter:  `db.balances.find({ active: true, credits: { $lt: safeLimit } }); // safeLimit validated separately and passed as a bound parameter`\n",
  "Protecting secrets in Node.js applications\nWHAT: Risk of exposing API keys and credentials by hardcoding them in source code or committing them in plain text to version control.\nWHY: Leaked secrets allow attackers to access external services, data stores, and infrastructure, leading to data theft, service abuse, or full environment compromise.\nHOW: Store secrets in environment variables (process.env) instead of code, factor all credentials out of the codebase, and use encryption (e.g., cryptr) if secrets must be stored in source control; additionally, use tools like git-secrets to scan commits for accidental secret inclusion.\nEXAMPLE (JavaScript, insecure): const apiKey = 'hardcoded-key'; const blobService = azure.createBlobService(apiKey);\nEXAMPLE (JavaScript, secure): const apiKey = process.env.AZURE_STORAGE_KEY; const blobService = azure.createBlobService(apiKey);\n",
  "Prevent brute-force attacks on authentication and privileged routes\nWHAT: Missing or weak rate limiting on login and other privileged endpoints allows unlimited authentication attempts, enabling brute-force and dictionary attacks against usernames/passwords.\nWHY: Successful brute-force attacks can lead to unauthorized account access, privilege escalation, and full compromise of protected resources.\nHOW: Apply server-side rate limiting keyed by request properties (e.g., IP, username/email) to cap consecutive and daily failed attempts, and temporarily block or slow down abusive sources; ensure all authentication-like routes (not just /login) are covered.\nEXAMPLE: Before: Expose `/login` without any limit on POST attempts from the same IP/username. After: Enforce a maximum of N consecutive failed attempts per username+IP and M total failed attempts per IP per day, then block or delay further requests from those keys.\n",
  "Run Node.js Processes as Non-Root\nWHAT: Running Node.js (especially in Docker containers or on privileged ports like 80/443) as root allows any compromise in the app to immediately become a full root compromise of the host/container.\nWHY: If an attacker exploits a vulnerability while the process runs as root, they can gain total control (e.g., wipe disks, pivot, route traffic) instead of being constrained by limited user permissions.\nHOW: Run Node.js under an unprivileged user; in Docker, set `USER node` (or another non-root user) in the Dockerfile or use `-u node`, and avoid binding directly to privileged ports—use non-privileged ports with port forwarding (iptables) or a reverse proxy (nginx/apache) listening on 80/443.\nEXAMPLE:\nBefore (insecure Dockerfile): `FROM node:latest ... EXPOSE 80 ... CMD [\"node\",\"server.js\"]` (run container as default root, bind to 80).\nAfter (safer Dockerfile): `FROM node:latest ... EXPOSE 3000 ... USER node ... CMD [\"node\",\"server.js\"]` and front nginx on 80→3000.\n",
  "Limit request payload size to prevent DoS\nWHAT: Unbounded HTTP request body/payload sizes can cause excessive memory/CPU usage when parsing (e.g., JSON), leading to performance degradation or crashes.\nWHY: Attackers can send very large payloads to exhaust server resources, causing denial-of-service and instability for other users.\nHOW: Configure strict maximum body sizes in your web framework middleware and reverse proxy/web server; reject requests exceeding the limit with an appropriate status code (e.g., 413).\nEXAMPLE: In Express: `app.use(express.json({ limit: '300kb' }));` In nginx: `client_max_body_size 1m;`\n"
]